\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Spaced Practice in Vocabulary Instruction:\\A Classroom Field Study with Secondary Students}
\author{M.~Okafor and L.~Petrov}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Vocabulary knowledge is a persistent bottleneck in second-language
instruction, and the scheduling of practice is among its most studied
levers.
The spacing of study episodes --- distributed practice --- is a
natural candidate mechanism \cite{melton1970}.
Evidence from laboratory word-list designs suggests that learners
retain spaced items far better than massed items, even when total
study time is held constant \cite{baddeley1997}.

\section{Methods}
We recruited 212 secondary-school students across eight intact
English classes.
Half of the classes practised the target words in three spaced
sessions spread over two weeks;
the remainder covered the same words in a single massed session.
The primary outcome was cued recall on a delayed test administered
four weeks after the final practice session.

\section{Results}
Spaced classes recalled 58\% of the target words, against 34\% in
the massed classes.
The advantage of spacing was larger for low-frequency words and did
not interact with baseline reading ability.

\section{Discussion}
Spacing effects in our classrooms are sizeable and practically
consequential.
This pattern is consistent with the broader literature on retrieval practice.
Interleaving short quizzes into regular lessons deserves evaluation
as a low-cost intervention in language instruction.

